//@version=5
// The four finalists from the 143,820-configuration search, in one script with a preset switch.
// One script rather than four files so all four share IDENTICAL logic -- that is the only way to
// guarantee the differences between them are the parameters and nothing else.
//
// ================================ THE FOUR, AND THEIR NUMBERS ================================
//                                              train   US30-2026   US100-all   US100-2026
//   #1   e30/x20  2.0N  2u  adx25  vol1.0  tp1R   1.47      0.91        1.24        1.14
//   #5   e30/x20  2.5N  3u  adx25  vol1.2  tp1R   1.60      1.34        1.24        0.76
//   #8   e30/x20  2.5N  3u  adx15          tp2R   1.31      1.19        1.14        1.08   <-- the only one
//   #10  e10/x20  2.0N  2u  adx25 d1 v1.2  tp1R   1.58      0.71        1.08        0.73       that survived
//                                                                                              all three
// Trained on US30 15-minute BEFORE 2026-01-01. The three right-hand columns were never searched.
// **#8 is the only one positive on all three**, and it is the LOOSEST-gated of the four -- the
// three carrying ADX>=25 plus a volatility floor scored better on train and then fell over. That
// is the overfit signature, visible in this table without any statistics.
// #8 on US30 2026: n=309, +28.96 pts/trade, 31.1% wins, p 0.0013 against a selectivity control.
//
// NONE OF THE FOUR PASSES A FUNDED EVALUATION. Best P(pass) anywhere in the whole 143,820-cell
// search is 34.6% with 61.0% P(bust); #8 itself peaks at 19.8%. The constraint is the trade
// DISTRIBUTION -- 31% wins with 12-16 consecutive losers against a 4% trailing drawdown -- not the
// parameters. Full working in docs/ib/STUDY_MEGA_144K.md.
//
// ==================== WHERE PINE CANNOT BIT-MATCH THE RESEARCH ENGINE ====================
// The rules below are transcribed from `research/turtleshort/mirror.py::run`, the function the
// table above came from. Four differences are STRUCTURAL -- they come from Pine's order model or
// from a warm-up convention, not from a choice -- and pretending otherwise would be the dishonest
// part of a port:
//
//   1. LADDER TIMING -- THE ONE THAT MATTERS, AND IT IS LARGE. The engine adds rungs and RE-ANCHORS
//      THE STOP TO THE NEWEST FILL WITHIN A SINGLE BAR, so a bar that runs up through three rungs
//      and then reverses stops the engine out immediately at a stop that has jumped 1.0N higher.
//      Pine only learns a fill at that bar's close, so its exit order for the ladder bar is still
//      anchored to the previous fill and the trade survives. All remaining rungs are placed at
//      once below (their levels are deterministic: fill + k * 0.5N), which recovers the
//      several-fills-in-one-bar part, but the intrabar stop re-anchor CANNOT be expressed in Pine.
//      Direction: this script is the OPTIMISTIC one. Measured on NQ 15m, below.
//      The engine also adds on the ENTRY BAR itself; Pine cannot, because the rung levels depend
//      on a fill price that is unknown until that bar closes.
//   2. TARGET VERSUS STOP IN ONE BAR. The engine always takes the STOP when both sit inside a
//      bar's range -- a bar-level loop cannot know the order and the pessimistic reading is the
//      honest one. TradingView with Deep Backtesting and high detalization uses the ACTUAL
//      intrabar sequence, which is better information. Expect TradingView to be slightly kinder.
//   3. COSTS. The engine charges a flat points-per-fill (3.22 US30 points, set to 3.7% of the 2N
//      stop, the same fraction NQ pays) once per UNIT, covering the round turn. TradingView charges
//      commission plus slippage on EVERY order, so entry and exit are billed separately. Match the
//      FRACTION of the stop, not the number: commission + slippage, doubled for the round turn,
//      should come to about 3.7% of `atrMult * ATR(20)` on your instrument.
//   4. VOLATILITY-FLOOR WARM-UP. The gate divides ATR(20) by its own 200-bar average. The engine
//      starts that average at 50 bars, `ta.sma` needs the full 200, so on bars 50-199 of the chart
//      the volatility floor is OFF in the engine and blocks entry here. Affects #1, #5 and #10 at
//      the very start of a chart only; #8 has no volatility floor.
//
// ======================= WHAT THE PARITY TEST ACTUALLY MEASURED =======================
// `research/turtle15/pine_parity.py` re-implements THIS SCRIPT'S order model in Python -- orders
// placed at a bar's close, live from the next bar -- and runs it against mirror.run on the same
// bars, with the engine's tie-break, so the only surviving differences are order timing.
// NQ 15-minute, 70,685 bars, 1.72 pts/unit:
//
//   LADDER DISABLED (max_units = 1), which isolates every rule except the ladder:
//     preset   signals matched   exit bar identical   engine pts/tr   this script   pnl corr
//       #1          99.7%              96.8%              6.24            5.90       0.9957
//       #5         100.0%              94.4%              4.43            3.99       0.9979
//       #8          98.0%              92.3%              4.09            4.19       0.9917
//       #10         99.5%              96.2%              4.38            4.39       0.9979
//   That is the transcription check, and it passes: trigger, gates, System 1/2 precedence,
//   skip-after-winner, exit-channel selection, the frozen ATR, the target and the
//   no-signal-on-the-exit-bar rule all reproduce.
//
//   LADDER ENABLED, as the presets actually run:
//     preset   signals matched   engine pts/tr   this script   engine PF   this script PF
//       #1          94.8%             10.03         14.74        1.22          1.37
//       #5          95.3%              9.23         15.79        1.12          1.22
//       #8          95.3%              7.40         15.07        1.10          1.22
//       #10         96.4%              6.11         10.54        1.12          1.24
//   READ THIS BEFORE READING THE STRATEGY TESTER. Expect roughly 1.5-2x the engine's points per
//   trade purely from the ladder's within-bar stop re-anchor. The header table at the top came
//   from the ENGINE, so a better number here is not a better strategy -- it is this gap. Which of
//   the two is right in live trading is genuinely undecided at bar resolution: nobody moves a stop
//   to a fill they have not seen yet, which argues for this script, and nobody gets three rungs
//   filled on one 15-minute bar without the risk that came with them, which argues for the engine.
//   To compare like for like, set Max units = 1 in Custom.
//
//   SESSION WINDOW ON (entries 06:00-11:45 New York, flatten 12:00), ladder disabled -- the
//   transcription check for the feature added below:
//     preset   signals matched   exit bar identical   engine pts/tr   this script   pnl corr
//       #1         100.0%             70.3%             -1.13          -1.61       0.9961
//       #5         100.0%             50.6%             -2.95          -3.56       0.9940
//       #8          99.1%             66.0%             +4.01          +3.74       0.9893
//       #10        100.0%             59.3%             -1.04          -1.47       0.9968
//   The exit-bar column drops because the flatten fills one bar later here (market order at the
//   flatten bar's close, filling at the next open, against the engine's exit AT that close). It
//   costs 0.3-0.6 points per trade and the sign is against this script, not for it.
//
//   WHAT THE WINDOW DOES TO THE FOUR, ladder on, NQ 15m -- READ THIS BEFORE ENABLING IT:
//     preset   no session          06:00-11:45 + flatten 12:00
//       #1      +10.03  PF 1.22       -6.86  PF 0.84
//       #5       +9.23  PF 1.12       -8.14  PF 0.87
//       #8       +7.40  PF 1.10       +0.19  PF 1.00
//       #10      +6.11  PF 1.12       -9.81  PF 0.77
//   The morning window the search encoded destroys all four. That is consistent with what this
//   branch already found twice: the 07:00-11:00 window's own baseline is negative.
//
//   AND DO NOT READ A WINDOW SWEEP AS A DISCOVERY. Sweeping 34 windows on #8 puts 23 of them
//   positive with a best of +15.44 (10:30-14:00) against +7.40 for no session at all. That is a
//   selection over 34 partitions of the same data, with no matched control, on NQ rather than
//   the US30/US100 blocks the finalists were actually judged on -- the calendar-condition
//   lottery this repository has a standing rule against. The tell is #1: its best window scores
//   +10.11 against +10.03 for no session, so the "improvement" does not survive changing preset.
//   USE THE WINDOW FOR OPERATIONAL REASONS -- not holding overnight, a prop firm's flat-by rule,
//   a session you can actually watch. Do not use it as an edge.
// ======================================================================================
//
// Everything else is a faithful transcription, including the parts that are easy to get wrong:
//   * Both entry channels are always live. The 55-bar breakout is System 2; the e1-bar breakout is
//     System 1. System 2 takes precedence when both fire on one bar.
//   * The EXIT channel depends on which system entered: System 1 exits on the x1-bar low,
//     System 2 on the 20-bar low. Both are 20 in all four presets.
//   * SKIP-AFTER-WINNER applies to System 1 ONLY, and the skip consumes itself whether or not the
//     skipped signal would have won. A System 2 signal never consumes it.
//   * ATR IS FROZEN AT THE SIGNAL BAR and used for the stop distance, the ladder step and the
//     target for the whole life of the trade. It does not re-read.
//   * The stop RE-ANCHORS TO THE LATEST FILL on every add, and the working exit is the HIGHER of
//     that stop and the channel low.
//   * The target is measured from the RUNNING AVERAGE entry, so adding moves it.
//   * Channels EXCLUDE the current bar, or a breakout could never happen.
//   * NO NEW SIGNAL ON THE BAR A TRADE CLOSED. The engine resumes its scan at the bar AFTER the
//     exit bar, so the earliest possible re-entry is two bars later.
//   * The gate is read at the SIGNAL bar and never re-checked while the trade is open.
// ========================================================================================

strategy("Turtle 4 finalists (searched, 2026-judged)", overlay = true, pyramiding = 4,
     default_qty_type = strategy.fixed, default_qty_value = 1,
     initial_capital = 100000, calc_on_every_tick = false, process_orders_on_close = false,
     commission_type = strategy.commission.cash_per_order, commission_value = 0.90, slippage = 2)

// ===================== STRATEGY TESTER PROPERTIES =====================
//   Script execution   ON BAR CLOSE only -- see docs/ib/STUDY_TICK_RECALC.md.
//   Bar detalization   High, with Deep Backtesting.   Order execution delay  One tick.
//   Commission         CASH PER ORDER, per side, your broker's real number. A 3-unit ladder pays
//                      it six times. See structural difference 3 above.
//   Slippage           2 ticks. A stop fills INTO the move that triggered it.
//   Leverage Infinity  stops silent order rejection; then read DOLLAR drawdown, not %.
//   Read the TRADE COUNT and the COMMISSION LOAD before the P&L.
// ======================================================================

// Short preset keys on purpose: a long descriptive string repeated in eight comparisons is one
// typo away from silently selecting the wrong configuration.
preset = input.string("#8", "Finalist", options = ["#1", "#5", "#8", "#10", "Custom"],
     tooltip = "#1  e30/x20 2.0N 2u adx25 vol1.0 tp1R\n#5  e30/x20 2.5N 3u adx25 vol1.2 tp1R\n#8  e30/x20 2.5N 3u adx15 tp2R  -- the only one positive on all three held-back blocks\n#10 e10/x20 2.0N 2u adx25 dist1.0 vol1.2 tp1R")

gC     = "Custom only (ignored unless Finalist = Custom)"
cE1    = input.int(30,    "Entry channel (System 1)", minval = 2,   group = gC)
cX1    = input.int(20,    "Exit channel (System 1)",  minval = 2,   group = gC)
cMult  = input.float(2.5, "ATR stop multiple",        minval = 0.1, step = 0.1, group = gC)
cUnits = input.int(3,     "Max units",                minval = 1,   maxval = 4, group = gC)
cAdx   = input.float(15,  "ADX floor (0 = off)",      minval = 0,   step = 1,   group = gC)
cDist  = input.float(0,   "EMA100 distance floor, in ATR (0 = off)", minval = 0, step = 0.5, group = gC)
cVol   = input.float(0,   "ATR expansion floor (0 = off)", minval = 0, step = 0.1, group = gC)
cTp    = input.float(2,   "Take profit in R (0 = off)",    minval = 0, step = 0.5, group = gC)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- session window
// NOT PART OF ANY OF THE FOUR FINALISTS. All four were searched with the session flag OFF, so
// this is a NEW configuration that has never been through the out-of-sample judge. Default off,
// so the presets reproduce exactly as shipped. Turning it on invalidates the header table.
//
// It reproduces the search's own encoding (`finalists.py::mask_for`, `sess == 1`), which is two
// separate things, not one:
//   * an ENTRY WINDOW read at the SIGNAL bar -- `mod >= start and mod < flatten - one bar`. The
//     last bar is excluded because entry is at the NEXT bar's open, which would land on the
//     flatten itself.
//   * a HARD FLATTEN at `flatten`, which closes the position wherever it is.
// The window does NOT wrap past midnight, exactly as the search's expression did not.
//
// BARE `hour` AND `minute` ARE EXCHANGE TIME IN PINE -- Chicago for CME, so an hour off New York
// for part of the year and two hours for the rest. The research clock is New York, so every read
// below passes the timezone explicitly.
gS       = "Session window (off by default -- not part of any finalist)"
sessOn   = input.bool(false, "Restrict entries to a window and flatten", group = gS)
startHH  = input.int(6,  "Entry window start -- hour (New York)", minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = gS)
startMM  = input.int(0,  "Entry window start -- minute",          minval = 0, maxval = 59, group = gS)
flatHH   = input.int(12, "Flatten -- hour (New York)",            minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = gS)
flatMM   = input.int(0,  "Flatten -- minute",                     minval = 0, maxval = 59, group = gS)

gF      = "Fixed by the search (shared by all four)"
entry2  = input.int(55,    "Entry channel (System 2)", minval = 2, group = gF)
exit2   = input.int(20,    "Exit channel (System 2)",  minval = 2, group = gF)
atrLen  = input.int(20,    "ATR length",               minval = 2, group = gF)
pyrStep = input.float(0.5, "Ladder step, in N",     minval = 0.0, step = 0.05, group = gF)
skipWin = input.bool(true, "Skip after a winning trade (System 1 only)", group = gF)
unitQty = input.float(1.0, "Contracts per Turtle unit", minval = 0.01, step = 0.01, group = gF)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- preset resolution
entry1 = switch preset
    "#1"  => 30
    "#5"  => 30
    "#8"  => 30
    "#10" => 10
    => cE1

exit1 = switch preset
    "#1"  => 20
    "#5"  => 20
    "#8"  => 20
    "#10" => 20
    => cX1

atrMult = switch preset
    "#1"  => 2.0
    "#5"  => 2.5
    "#8"  => 2.5
    "#10" => 2.0
    => cMult

maxUnits = switch preset
    "#1"  => 2
    "#5"  => 3
    "#8"  => 3
    "#10" => 2
    => cUnits

adxMin = switch preset
    "#1"  => 25.0
    "#5"  => 25.0
    "#8"  => 15.0
    "#10" => 25.0
    => cAdx

distMin = switch preset
    "#1"  => 0.0
    "#5"  => 0.0
    "#8"  => 0.0
    "#10" => 1.0
    => cDist

volMin = switch preset
    "#1"  => 1.0
    "#5"  => 1.2
    "#8"  => 0.0
    "#10" => 1.2
    => cVol

tpR = switch preset
    "#1"  => 1.0
    "#5"  => 1.0
    "#8"  => 2.0
    "#10" => 1.0
    => cTp

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- series
// Channels EXCLUDE the current bar -- ta.highest(high, n) includes bar n's own high, so a high
// could never exceed it and the signal would never fire.
atrN    = ta.atr(atrLen)                      // Wilder's RMA of TR, matching mirror.wilder_atr
chanHi1 = ta.highest(high, entry1)[1]
chanHi2 = ta.highest(high, entry2)[1]
chanLo1 = ta.lowest(low,  exit1)[1]
chanLo2 = ta.lowest(low,  exit2)[1]
ema100  = ta.ema(close, 100)
atrLong = ta.sma(atrN, 200)
// ta.dmi returns [+DI, -DI, ADX] IN THAT ORDER -- the first element is +DI, not ADX.
[_pdi, _mdi, adxVal] = ta.dmi(14, 14)
distEma = atrN > 0 ? (close - ema100) / atrN : na
volRat  = atrLong > 0 ? atrN / atrLong : na

// New York minutes-of-day, matching `fastbars.mod` (verified equal, DST and all).
modNY  = hour(time, "America/New_York") * 60 + minute(time, "America/New_York")
startM = startHH * 60 + startMM
flatM  = flatHH * 60 + flatMM
barMin = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) / 60
// Read at the SIGNAL bar, so it joins the gate rather than the exit.
inWin  = not sessOn or (modNY >= startM and modNY < flatM - barMin)
atFlat = sessOn and modNY >= flatM

// A floor of 0 means the gate is OFF -- exactly as the search encoded it (`if row[..] > 0`).
okAdx  = adxMin  <= 0 or (not na(adxVal)  and adxVal  >= adxMin)
okDist = distMin <= 0 or (not na(distEma) and distEma >= distMin)
okVol  = volMin  <= 0 or (not na(volRat)  and volRat  >= volMin)
// The engine's own precondition is ATR only; each channel is checked separately with its signal.
ready  = not na(atrN) and atrN > 0
gateOk = ready and okAdx and okDist and okVol and inWin

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- state
var float sigAtr    = na    // ATR FROZEN AT THE SIGNAL BAR: stop, ladder and target all read this
var float lastFill  = na
var float nextAdd   = na
var int   sysOn     = 0
var int   unitsOn   = 0
var int   lastCnt   = 0
var bool  lastWin   = false
var float entEquity = na
var int   exitBar   = -1    // the bar a trade last closed on; no new signal is allowed on it

// EVERY var-STATE BLOCK IS GUARDED, AND SO IS THE EXIT CALL. Guarding only part of a coupled set
// desynchronises it rather than partly fixing it -- see docs/ib/STUDY_TICK_RECALC.md.
if barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.position_size == 0 and lastCnt > 0
    // A trade just closed. strategy.netprofit is CLOSED-trade profit, so comparing it with the
    // value captured at the first fill is exactly the engine's `last_win = pnl > 0`.
    lastWin  := strategy.netprofit > nz(entEquity)
    exitBar  := bar_index
    sysOn    := 0
    unitsOn  := 0
    sigAtr   := na
    lastFill := na
    nextAdd  := na
    lastCnt  := 0

if barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.opentrades > lastCnt
    // A fill happened: re-anchor the stop and the next rung to THAT fill, as the engine does.
    lastFill := strategy.opentrades.entry_price(strategy.opentrades - 1)
    if lastCnt == 0
        entEquity := strategy.netprofit
    nextAdd := lastFill + pyrStep * sigAtr
    unitsOn := strategy.opentrades
    lastCnt := strategy.opentrades

flat = strategy.position_size == 0

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- entries
// Both channels are live. System 2 (55-bar) takes precedence when both fire on the same bar.
sig2 = not na(chanHi2) and high > chanHi2
sig1 = not na(chanHi1) and high > chanHi1

// `bar_index > exitBar` reproduces the engine resuming its scan at j+1, never on the exit bar.
// The bracket goes on WITH the entry so it is live during the ENTRY BAR. `loss`/`profit` are
// measured by Pine from the actual fill, which is exactly what the engine anchors to while the
// position is one unit -- and it is one unit for the whole entry bar. Where `stop` and `loss` are
// both given Pine takes the SMALLER LOSS, which for a long is the HIGHER level: that is the
// engine's max(ATR stop, channel low), reproduced exactly. Its channel leg reads the SIGNAL bar's
// channel, one bar staler than the engine's. Without this, 4.4-13.0% of trades -- the ones the
// engine stops out on the entry bar, averaging -33 to -118 points -- ran on unprotected.
// `loss` and `profit` are declared `series int`, so the tick counts are cast, not left float.
lossTk = int(math.max(1, math.round(atrMult * atrN / syminfo.mintick)))
tpTk   = tpR <= 0 ? na : int(math.max(1, math.round(tpR * atrMult * atrN / syminfo.mintick)))
if barstate.isconfirmed and flat and gateOk and bar_index > exitBar
    if sig2
        sigAtr := atrN
        sysOn  := 2
        strategy.entry("S2", strategy.long, qty = unitQty)
        strategy.exit("x0", from_entry = "S2", stop = chanLo2, loss = lossTk, profit = tpTk)
    else if sig1
        if skipWin and lastWin
            // the skip consumes itself whether or not the skipped signal would have won
            lastWin := false
        else
            sigAtr := atrN
            sysOn  := 1
            strategy.entry("S1", strategy.long, qty = unitQty)
            strategy.exit("x0", from_entry = "S1", stop = chanLo1, loss = lossTk, profit = tpTk)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- ladder
// STOP orders at the rungs, so a fill is AT the level -- the engine fills at `nxt`, not at the
// bar's high. EVERY remaining rung rests simultaneously, because the engine's rung sequence is
// deterministic: each fill lands exactly on its rung, so rung k is lastFill + k * pyrStep * N.
// That recovers the engine's several-fills-in-one-bar behaviour. What it cannot recover is the
// engine re-anchoring the STOP to each of those fills within the same bar -- header note 1.
ladderOn = not flat and unitsOn > 0 and unitsOn < maxUnits and pyrStep > 0 and not na(lastFill) and not na(sigAtr)
if barstate.isconfirmed and ladderOn
    for k = 1 to maxUnits - unitsOn
        strategy.entry("add" + str.tostring(unitsOn + k), strategy.long, qty = unitQty, stop = lastFill + k * pyrStep * sigAtr)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- exit
// Working stop = the HIGHER of the fill-anchored ATR stop and the channel low, because falling
// price reaches that first. The channel depends on which SYSTEM entered.
atrStop   = na(lastFill) or na(sigAtr) ? na : lastFill - atrMult * sigAtr
chanExit  = sysOn == 1 ? chanLo1 : chanLo2
exitLevel = na(atrStop) ? chanExit : (na(chanExit) ? atrStop : math.max(atrStop, chanExit))
// The target is measured from the RUNNING AVERAGE entry, so adding a unit moves it.
tpBase    = strategy.position_avg_price
tpLevel   = tpR <= 0 or flat or na(sigAtr) or na(tpBase) ? na : tpBase + tpR * atrMult * sigAtr

if barstate.isconfirmed and not flat and lastCnt > 0 and not na(exitLevel)
    // "x0" was the entry-bar bracket and covered one unit; "x" covers the whole position from
    // here on. Cancel the first before placing the second or both rest and the position can be
    // closed twice.
    strategy.cancel("x0")
    strategy.exit("x", stop = exitLevel, limit = tpLevel)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- flatten
// LAST, deliberately: it cancels the ladder and exit orders the blocks above just placed, and if
// it ran before them they would simply be replaced. Cancelling the LADDER is the part that is
// easy to miss -- a resting rung order left alive after the position closes opens a NEW position
// outside the window.
//
// The engine exits at the CLOSE of the first bar with mod >= flatten. `strategy.close_all` is a
// market order placed at that bar's close, so it fills at the NEXT bar's OPEN. One bar later and
// at a different price; the size of that is measured in the header. It cannot be fixed without
// `process_orders_on_close = true`, which would change how every other order in the script fills.
if barstate.isconfirmed and atFlat
    strategy.cancel_all()
    if not flat
        strategy.close_all(comment = "flatten")

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- plots
plot(chanHi1, "Entry 1", color = color.new(color.aqua, 35))
plot(chanHi2, "Entry 2 (55)", color = color.new(color.blue, 45))
plot(chanLo1, "Exit 1", color = color.new(color.red, 65))
plot(chanLo2, "Exit 2 (20)", color = color.new(color.purple, 65))
plot(flat ? na : exitLevel, "Working stop", color = color.fuchsia, style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(tpLevel, "Target", color = color.new(color.lime, 0), style = plot.style_linebr)

onTf     = timeframe.period == "15"
survived = preset == "#8"
lbl1     = survived ? "positive on all three held-back blocks" : "NEGATIVE on at least one held-back block"
lbl2     = onTf ? "15m chart" : "NOT 15m -- searched on 15m only"
var table hud = table.new(position.top_right, 1, 4, border_width = 1)
if barstate.islast
    table.cell(hud, 0, 0, "Finalist " + preset, text_color = color.white, bgcolor = survived ? color.new(color.teal, 20) : color.new(color.orange, 20))
    table.cell(hud, 0, 1, lbl1, text_color = color.white, bgcolor = survived ? color.new(color.teal, 40) : color.new(color.red, 25))
    table.cell(hud, 0, 2, lbl2, text_color = color.white, bgcolor = onTf ? color.new(color.teal, 40) : color.red)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 3, "FUNDED EVAL: best P(pass) in the whole search 34.6%, P(bust) 61%", text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.red, 15))
